Kevin Wayne  <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> wrote:
>Quoth David Damerell on this day of 19 Apr 2004 11:49:15 +0100 (BST):
>>I can't understand why people are so eager to preserve the early WoW
>>crapshoot. Yes, NetHack games will vary wildly in difficulty; that doesn't
>>mean that that variation should be needlessly increased. Why not just pick
>>one in 500 starting characters and let them win immediately?
>Whether to keep a WoW's maximum charges at 7 and whether to allow "the
>early WoW crapshoot" are two separate questions.

Not entirely separate, no. The effect of getting an early WoW is obviously
increased when it represents more wishes; ie, reducing the number of
wishes decreases the effect of winning the crapshoot without raising the
ugly head of item generation depths.

Also, even a midgame WoW could make the game trivial up to the Castle
wand...

>Since you like the reductio ad absurdum, David, on your logic why not
>simply eliminate wishes from the game entirely? They're highly
>overpowered.

I don't think it follows, merely because something is powerful, that it is
overpowered. Something has to be the best item in the game.

[In particular, a 3-shot Castle wand would not increase the luck factor,
because it would always be there!]

Essentially, I feel that there is a right amount of randomness in the
game. No randomness would obviously be uninteresting; and Angband goes too
far in this direction, with the rarity of out-of-depth monsters and loot.
Conversely, a totally random game would be equally uninteresting - you'd
start the game and immediately be told when and where you died.

By and large I think the game gets it about right; the occasional gnome
with a wand of death is the price we pay for having a wide variety of
interesting situations. But the possibility of getting the full AK early
does not create any interesting situations.

>The early WoW possibility doesn't even come up that often, anyway.

I don't see that that argues against a change.
